Object.assign(window.deepArticles,{
  "western-succession-crisis":westernCampaignArticle({
    title:"Western Succession Crisis",eyebrow:"State-formation conflict · 1947–1949",infoboxKicker:"Collapse of the northwestern Raj",flag:westernSequenceDiagram,
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w5u6v writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:27:30-06:00 -->The western collapse of British authority produces Afghan occupation of Chagai and Quetta–Pishin, independent Kalat, an Iranian-backed Baloch confederation, sovereign Sindh at Karachi, and a Pakistani nationalist government along the Sibi corridor.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Grouping the founding conflicts into a 1947–49 “Western Succession Crisis” is compatible periodization.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w5u6w writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:27:30-06:00 -->The principal state-formation sequence, Afghan occupations, independent Sindh and Karachi, Kalat's separate survival, the Baloch confederation, and the Pakistan Emergency Government are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1947–49 crisis period, personal declarations and founding actors, and Iranian-financed Gwadar transfer are compatible synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact dates, individual battles, local accessions, occupation lines, casualty totals, and participating commanders remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"collapse",title:"Collapse of frontier authority",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w9c4a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:05:00-06:00 --><p>The collapse of British rule leaves several separate northwestern states and claimants rather than a unified Pakistan.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A simultaneous contest among directly administered districts, princely states, tribal governments, rail commands, municipalities, Muslim League committees, and neighboring kingdoms is compatible state-formation context.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"afghanistan",title:"Afghan occupation",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w9c4b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:05:00-06:00 --><p><a href='afghanistan.html'>Afghanistan</a> takes Chagai and Quetta–Pishin during the western collapse.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Pashtun national justification, treatment of Chagai as depth toward Iran and Kandahar, differing garrison burdens, and the relative difficulty of holding Quetta are incorporated strategic interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"kalat",title:"Independent Kalat",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w9c4c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:05:00-06:00 --><p><a href='kalat.html'>Kalat</a> appears as a separate khanate centered on Kalat and enters conflict with Balochistan.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A declaration by <a href='ahmad-yar-khan.html'>Ahmad Yar Khan</a>, claims over Sarawan, Jhalawan, Kachhi, and subordinate principalities, and a coordinated rejection by Makran, Kharan, and Las Bela are incorporated founding reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"baloch",title:"Iranian-aligned Balochistan",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w9c4d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:05:00-06:00 --><p>An Iranian-backed Balochistani polity initially centered at <a href='gwadar.html'>Gwadar</a> forms from Makran and Kharan, with Las Bela initially associated and Chagai entering later.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A formal coastal confederation of rulers and a bounded dynastic-client doctrine opposed to pan-Baloch revolution are incorporated institutional interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"gwadar",title:"Working Gwadar transfer",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w9c4e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:05:00-06:00 --><p>The author establishes Gwadar as the Balochistani polity's initial center but does not describe how it leaves Omani control.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> An Iranian-financed <a href='gwadar-transfer.html'>transfer from Oman</a>, conditioned on Iranian port access, military and commercial privileges, limits on foreign basing, and customs repayment, is incorporated diplomatic reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"pakistan",title:"Pakistan at Sibi",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w9c4f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:05:00-06:00 --><p>Pakistani nationalists hold a weak state around Sibi and the remaining Chief Commissioner's Province districts, from which they attempt to restore a wider Pakistan.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A formal <a href='pakistan-emergency-government.html'>Pakistan Emergency Government</a> created by specified League officials, officers, refugees, and railway personnel, with a fixed claim list and exact Loralai–Zhob core, is incorporated institutional reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"sindh",title:"Sindh and Karachi",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w9c4g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:05:00-06:00 --><p><a href='sindh.html'>Sindh</a> is an independent state controlling the lower Indus and Karachi and later sells weapons among the western belligerents.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Immediate control of specified banks, yards, insurance, and rail institutions, opposition to restoration of Pakistan, and a deliberate balance-of-power doctrine are compatible economic and diplomatic reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"outcome",title:"Western successor centers",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w9c4h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:05:00-06:00 --><p>The collapse leaves Afghan occupation, independent Kalat, Iranian-backed Balochistan, Pakistani nationalists around Sibi, and sovereign Sindh as durable rival centers.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Fixing that outcome by 1949 and treating the next phase as formally named <a href='first-baloch-wars.html'>First Baloch Wars</a> is incorporated periodization.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"first-baloch-wars.html",kicker:"Immediate continuation",label:"First Baloch Wars"},{href:"pakistan-emergency-government.html",kicker:"National claimant",label:"Pakistan Emergency Government"},{href:"baloch-confederated-state.html",kicker:"Coastal claimant",label:"Baloch Confederated State"},{href:"western-indian-wars.html",kicker:"Long conflict system",label:"Western Indian Wars"}],
    facts:[["Period","1947–1949"],["Theater","Indus, Baloch, Quetta, and frontier regions"],["Principal outside powers","Afghanistan and Iran"],["Sindhi gain","Karachi and lower Indus"],["Pakistani core","Sibi, Loralai, and Zhob"],["Baloch centers","Kalat and the coastal confederation"],["Major diplomatic act","Iranian-financed transfer of Gwadar"],["Result","Five durable rival centers"]]
  }),

  "first-baloch-wars":westernCampaignArticle({
    title:"First Baloch Wars",eyebrow:"Baloch succession wars · 1949–1955",infoboxKicker:"Kalat–Balochistan conflict",flag:westernSequenceDiagram,
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b6v7w writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:27:30-06:00 -->Kalat and the Iranian-backed Baloch state fight repeatedly over Las Bela, Makran approaches, passes, wells, and routes toward Gwadar while Afghanistan, Pakistan, Iran, and Sindh intervene according to their own frontier interests.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The collective title “First Baloch Wars,” 1949–55 bounds, and one opening phase covering Kachhi, Chagai, and Quetta are compatible campaign organization.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b6v7x writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:27:30-06:00 -->Kalat–Balochistan territorial conflict, Afghan occupation of Quetta and Chagai, Iranian support, Sindhi arms brokerage, and Pakistani attempts against Quetta are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1949–55 period, First Baloch Wars title, Iranian escalation sequence, Afghan consolidation phase, and exact relation among the campaigns are compatible synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Individual war names, campaign phases, commanders, battlefields, losses, and interim lines remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"claims",title:"Working sovereignty claims",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f1b3a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:07:00-06:00 --><p><a href='baloch-confederated-state.html'>Balochistan</a> and <a href='kalat.html'>Kalat</a> are established rivals over Baloch territories.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A modern confederated-national claim by Makran, Kharan, Las Bela, and Gwadar opposed to Kalat's doctrine of lawful princely subordination is incorporated legal reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"las-bela",title:"Las Bela and the route to the sea",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f1b3b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:07:00-06:00 --><p><a href='las-bela.html'>Las Bela</a> is contested and eventually gives Kalat a route to the sea and a stronger regional position.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Its formal status as a founding confederate member, and the specific value of fishing, smuggling, arms access, and dynastic proof, are compatible economic and political interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"makran",title:"Makran and the western coast",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f1b3c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:07:00-06:00 --><p>Iran supports the Balochistani polity centered on Gwadar and based around Makran and Kharan, while Kalat attacks when its rival is overextended.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Iranian prevention of total conquest and a warfare pattern governed by roads, wells, ports, and dynastic loyalties are incorporated operational synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"afghanistan",title:"Afghan-held northern Balochistan",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f1b3d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:07:00-06:00 --><p>Afghanistan initially holds Chagai and Quetta–Pishin, while Balochistani forces periodically raid into Afghanistan as demonstrations before returning south.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A formal Southern Frontier Administration, Iranian intelligence support, fixed garrisons, and a distinct consolidation phase are incorporated institutional and campaign reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"pakistan",title:"Pakistani attempts on Quetta",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f1b3e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:07:00-06:00 --><p>Pakistani nationalists based around Sibi periodically take or attempt to take Quetta.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The objective of gaining a recognized capital and military center, the weakness of specified early attacks, and their classification as prehistory to a later <a href='quetta-campaigns.html'>campaign cycle</a> are incorporated chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"sindh",title:"Sindhi arms trade",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f1b3f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:07:00-06:00 --><p><a href='sindh.html'>Sindh</a> often sells weapons to every side and sometimes supports Kalat or Balochistan.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Banks, repair yards, and brokers deliberately shifting credit and matériel toward the weakest participant to prevent Baloch or Afghan dominance are compatible balance-of-power reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"warfare",title:"Mobile frontier warfare",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f1b3g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:07:00-06:00 --><p>The western states use modern mounted infantry because they lack the resources for full motorization, with limited trucks and occasional armored vehicles alongside horses.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Repeated seizure of towns, passes, wells, rail stops, and roads without durable control, together with a standard mix of levies and artillery, is incorporated operational texture.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"The dispute survives the war",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b7w8x writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:27:30-06:00 --><p>No common peace closes the period. Iranian support makes Balochistan durable. Kalat remains independent, Afghanistan holds the southern heights, Pakistan persists at Sibi, and Sindh profits from the division. Later wars inherit the unresolved territorial claims.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"western-succession-crisis.html",kicker:"Founding crisis",label:"Western Succession Crisis"},{href:"quetta-campaigns.html",kicker:"Later campaign cycle",label:"Quetta Campaigns"},{href:"second-las-bela-war.html",kicker:"Later Las Bela conflict",label:"Second Las Bela War"},{href:"third-balochistan-war.html",kicker:"Decisive successor war",label:"Third Balochistan War"}],
    facts:[["Period","1949–1955"],["Principal belligerents","Kalat and Baloch Confederated State"],["Contested regions","Las Bela, Makran, Kachhi, Chagai, Quetta–Pishin"],["Balochistan patron","Iran"],["Afghan position","Quetta and Chagai consolidated"],["Pakistan objective","Quetta"],["Commercial intermediary","Sindh"],["Result","Claims hardened; no stable settlement"]]
  }),

  "quetta-campaigns":westernCampaignArticle({
    title:"Quetta campaigns",eyebrow:"Pakistan–Afghan urban campaigns · 1957–1960",infoboxKicker:"Quetta cycle",flag:westernSequenceDiagram,
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-q1n2p writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:25:02-06:00 -->The Pakistan Emergency Government repeatedly attempts to seize Quetta and sometimes succeeds temporarily. The author tentatively proposes that Pakistan eventually holds Quetta–Pishin with its Sibi-region core, while Chagai passes to Balochistan.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Defining a 1957–60 Quetta campaign cycle that creates a durable state with Quetta as its effective capital is incorporated chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-q1n2q writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:25:02-06:00 -->Pakistan's recurring Quetta operations and occasional seizure of the city are established; final Pakistani consolidation of Quetta–Pishin is an authorial proposal phrased tentatively, while Chagai's eventual inclusion in Balochistan is established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1957–60 period, Loralai–Zhob territorial package, multiple changes of possession, capital role, and an Afghan strategic exchange for an eastern protected frontier are incorporated synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Final control, number of offensives, dates, withdrawal terms, battles, commanders, and casualties remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"symbol",title:"Working importance of Quetta",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-q7c5a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:09:00-06:00 --><p>Administrative buildings, depots, barracks, roads, urban population, and provincial symbolism make Quetta a plausible center for Pakistani nationalists based around Sibi.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author establishes attempts to take the city but does not state their institutional motivation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"afghan",title:"Working Afghan occupation",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-q7c5b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:09:00-06:00 --><p>Afghanistan initially occupies much of Quetta–Pishin.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Its interest in depth around Kandahar, proof of a southern claim, permanent garrison requirements, supply difficulty, and problems governing a mixed modern city are incorporated strategic interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"pakistan",title:"Working Pakistani operations",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-q7c5c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:09:00-06:00 --><p>Pakistani forces move from their Sibi-region base and on occasion take Quetta.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A Sibi–Loralai–Zhob operational system of colonial officers, railway troops, frontier recruits, mounted rifles, artillery, mechanization, and control of the Bolan approaches is incorporated force reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"cycle",title:"Working campaign cycle",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-q7c5d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:09:00-06:00 --><p>Repeated Pakistani attempts and occasional occupations are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Multiple formal changes of possession, overextended supply routes, Afghan counterpressure, local bargaining, mutual exhaustion, and successive interim arrangements are compatible campaign reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Chagai remains separate",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-q7c5e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:09:00-06:00 --><p><a href='chagai.html'>Chagai</a> eventually leaves Afghan control and joins the Balochistani polity, keeping it outside the Pakistani core.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A final border that prevents any Pakistani route to Iran depends on the still-open extent of Pakistani consolidation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"capital",title:"Working move from Sibi to Quetta",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-q7c5f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:09:00-06:00 --><p>The author tentatively proposes that Pakistan eventually holds Quetta and its surrounding former Chief Commissioner's Province districts.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Quetta becoming the effective capital and military center while Sibi remains a transport hinge and founding symbol is incorporated institutional development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"afghanistan-loss",title:"Working Afghan withdrawal",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-q7c5g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:09:00-06:00 --><p>Afghanistan losing Quetta and deliberately turning east toward a cheaper <a href='afghan-protected-frontier.html'>Peshawar–Waziristan protected system</a> is incorporated strategic synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Afghan influence farther east is established, but no exchange or withdrawal decision is.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Working Pakistani consolidation",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-q7c5h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:09:00-06:00 --><p>A durable Pakistani state centered on Quetta–Pishin, Sibi, Loralai, and Zhob, capable of later Punjabi attacks, is a compatible realization of the author's tentative proposal.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Final borders, capital status, and institutional durability remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"pakistan-emergency-government.html",kicker:"Victorious state",label:"Pakistan Emergency Government"},{href:"afghanistan.html",kicker:"Displaced occupier",label:"Kingdom of Afghanistan"},{href:"harappa-campaigns.html",kicker:"Eastern campaign cycle",label:"Harappa Campaigns"},{href:"western-indian-wars.html",kicker:"Wider system",label:"Western Indian Wars"}],
    facts:[["Working period","1957–1960"],["Location","Quetta–Pishin and Sibi approaches"],["Established operations","Repeated Pakistani attempts and occasional occupation"],["Initial holder","Afghanistan"],["Pakistani base","Sibi region; exact districts open"],["Author-proposed result","Pakistan probably consolidates Quetta–Pishin"],["Established excluded territory","Chagai joins Balochistan"],["Working strategic consequence","Quetta becomes effective capital"]]
  }),

  "harappa-campaigns":westernCampaignArticle({
    title:"Harappa campaigns",eyebrow:"Pakistan–Khalistan campaigns · 1964–1969",infoboxKicker:"Lower-Punjab campaign cycle",flag:westernSequenceDiagram,
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h2p3q writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:25:02-06:00 -->Pakistan repeatedly attacks into southern Khalistan during the 1950s and 1960s and on occasion reaches or occupies Harappa before withdrawing.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Harappa's inherited geography makes it a railway and road town rather than a river port.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> A discrete 1964–69 campaign cycle ending when Lahore concentrates against the Pakistani supply corridor is compatible operational chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h2p3r writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:25:02-06:00 -->Recurring Pakistani offensives, temporary occupations of Harappa, eventual withdrawal, and the resulting Khalistani hostility are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Harappa's non-maritime railway geography is inherited fact.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The 1964–69 period, fortified-depot role, exact Khalistani recovery mechanism, and a standardized recurring campaign pattern are compatible synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Individual campaign names, exact approaches, occupations, commanders, losses, and any Khalistani counterclaims on Pakistani territory remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"claim",title:"Pakistan's Punjab claim",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h3q4s writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:25:02-06:00 --><p>Pakistan's national maps include Lahore and the Muslim inheritance of Punjab. An attack toward Harappa extends the Quetta-based state's claim beyond its Baloch frontier core and preserves the possibility of national expansion.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"harappa",title:"A railway town, not a river port",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h6r8a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:11:00-06:00 --><p>Harappa is a railway and road town rather than a river port.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Its role as a fortified node near southern Khalistani control, the last secure link toward lower-Indus trade, and a logistical and symbolic objective is incorporated campaign geography.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"pakistani",title:"Working Pakistani advance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h6r8b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:11:00-06:00 --><p>Pakistan repeatedly advances toward Harappa and sometimes reaches or occupies it.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Rail troops, mounted rifles, truck-borne artillery, frontier formations, limited armor, rapid seizure before Khalistani mobilization, and dependence on a narrow supply route are incorporated force and operational detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"khalistani",title:"Working Khalistani recovery",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h6r8c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:11:00-06:00 --><p><a href='khalistan.html'>Khalistan</a> ultimately retains Harappa and becomes hostile toward Pakistan because of recurring attacks.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A larger population, superior Punjab transport, Lahore-centered command, more coherent army, and attacks on the penetration's shoulders are incorporated explanations for recovery.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"sindh",title:"Working Sindhi balance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h6r8d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:11:00-06:00 --><p>Sindh sometimes supports different western belligerents and often sells arms throughout the conflict system.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Supplying Pakistan when Khalistan threatens and then restricting credit or transit when Pakistan might retain Harappa is incorporated balance-of-power extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"pattern",title:"Working recurring pattern",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h6r8e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:11:00-06:00 --><p>Pakistan attacks Harappa repeatedly during the 1950s and 1960s, sometimes occupies it, and later withdraws.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Formal restoration proclamations, Khalistani concentration, logistical pressure, and a standardized pattern of tactical demonstration followed by strategic failure are incorporated campaign synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"mounted",title:"Mounted mobility on the Punjab edge",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h6r8f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:11:00-06:00 --><p>Modern mounted infantry is an established feature of the western wars, using horses for movement and fighting principally with modern automatic weapons.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Screening roads, raiding supply traffic, reconnaissance around Harappa, artillery and radio support, and a small motorized tail are compatible application to this campaign.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Southern hostility",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h6r8g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:11:00-06:00 --><p>Repeated Pakistani attacks on Harappa leave Khalistan hostile toward the Pakistani state.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Harappa becoming a heavily prepared frontier symbol and Pakistan's failure demonstrating the limits of national claims without economic depth are incorporated legacy interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"pakistan-emergency-government.html",kicker:"Attacking state",label:"Pakistan Emergency Government"},{href:"khalistan.html",kicker:"Defending state",label:"Khalistan"},{href:"modern-mounted-warfare-western-india.html",kicker:"Characteristic force",label:"Modern Mounted Warfare"},{href:"quetta-campaigns.html",kicker:"Western campaign cycle",label:"Quetta Campaigns"}],
    facts:[["Working period","1964–1969"],["Location","Harappa and lower Punjab"],["Attacker","Pakistan Emergency Government"],["Defender","Khalistan"],["Working objective","Symbolic and logistical Punjab foothold"],["Harappa's role","Railway town; working fortified-depot role"],["Occupation","Temporary on several occasions"],["Result","Khalistan retains the frontier"]]
  }),

  "modern-mounted-warfare-western-india":westernCampaignArticle({
    title:"Modern mounted warfare in western India",eyebrow:"Military doctrine and logistics · 1950s onward",infoboxKicker:"Horse-mounted infantry in the machine age",flag:mountedWarfareDiagram,
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m7w8x writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:27:30-06:00 -->Western Indian armies use horses for rapid movement because their states cannot afford full motorization and much of the frontier lacks dependable roads and fuel distribution. These are modern mounted infantry armed with automatic weapons: they ride, dismount, and fight on foot rather than serving as revived shock cavalry.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m7w8z writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:27:30-06:00 -->Widespread mounted rifles, modern dismounted tactics, mixed horse–truck–armor columns, local horse stocks, and the operational rationale of poverty and sparse infrastructure are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Three standardized formation types, formal remount institutions, breed allocations by state, detailed vulnerabilities, and rural economic consequences are compatible military synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Unit names, tables of organization, breed standards, and complete state inventories remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"why",title:"Why the horse remains useful",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m2h7a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:13:00-06:00 --><p>The western states lack the resources for full motorization, so locally available horses provide useful rapid movement where the alternative is often marching infantry.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Weak tax bases, sparse roads, imported fuel, inadequate workshops, and a formal choice among walking, unreliable trucks, and countryside skills are compatible material explanation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"rifles",title:"Mounted rifles",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m2h7b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:13:00-06:00 --><p>These troops are modern mounted infantry rather than lancers or hussars. They use AK-pattern rifles from horseback when useful or ride to a position and dismount to fight as infantry.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Light machine guns, grenades, rocket launchers, mortars, radios, pack ammunition, and crew-served weapons are incorporated equipment detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"frontier",title:"Working frontier roles",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m2h7c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:13:00-06:00 --><p>Reconnaissance, screening, pursuit, convoy escort, raids, patrols, militia suppression, and limited mounted fire form a compatible role set for the established mounted infantry.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The transcript establishes rapid mobility and dismounted combat but no formal frontier-cavalry doctrine.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"contingents",title:"Working local contingents",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m2h7d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:13:00-06:00 --><p>Regiments supplied by a khan, prince, tribal council, or local commander, retaining local officers, horses, loyalties, and the capacity to change sides, are incorporated organizational extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No standard contingent system or central financing rule is directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"mixed",title:"Mixed western columns",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m2h7e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:13:00-06:00 --><p>The author identifies a horse-mounted column moving beside an aging tank as a defining western-war image, with local states possessing some trucks and limited armor.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A standard combination of mounted screens, ammunition trucks, armored cars, tanks, pack animals, engineers, guides, and auxiliaries is incorporated force reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"remount",title:"The remount economy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m8x9a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:27:30-06:00 --><p>In the working institutional reconstruction, states maintain depots, subsidized stallions, veterinary schools, purchasing fairs, branding, registration, and emergency requisition. Balochi stock serves the southwest, Waziri and Afghan strains the frontier, Punjabi horses Khalistan, and Marwari and related breeds Rajputana.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>A district's military value may be measured in serviceable mounts after harvest. Raiding a herd can immobilize a formation without defeating it in battle.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"cost",title:"Working horse logistics",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m2h7f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:13:00-06:00 --><p>Water, fodder, farriers, saddles, veterinarians, remounts, disease control, drought risk, and rural household burdens are compatible logistical consequences of sustained mounted warfare.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The canon transcript does not specify an administrative system or economic burden.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Working limits",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m2h7g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:13:00-06:00 --><p>Vulnerability to aircraft, wire, machine guns, fortifications, water denial, and fodder attacks, together with continuing dependence on ammunition, radio batteries, artillery, and medical supply, is compatible military analysis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The directly established advantage is mobility without full motorization.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"western-indian-wars.html",kicker:"Parent conflict system",label:"Western Indian Wars"},{href:"harappa-campaigns.html",kicker:"Punjab example",label:"Harappa Campaigns"},{href:"second-las-bela-war.html",kicker:"Armored and mounted example",label:"Second Las Bela War"},{href:"rajputana.html",kicker:"Major mounted tradition",label:"Rajputana"}],
    facts:[["Established period","1950s–1960s; later persistence open"],["Established primary role","Rapid operational movement without full motorization"],["Established tactical method","Ride and dismount to fight; limited fire from horseback"],["Established armament","Modern automatic rifles, including AK-pattern weapons"],["Established combined image","Horse-mounted troops beside limited trucks and armor"],["Formal troop types and organization","Working reconstruction"],["Remount and veterinary systems","Working reconstruction"],["Detailed vulnerabilities and economic burden","Working analysis"]]
  }),

  "kashmir-khalistan-war":westernCampaignArticle({
    title:"Kashmir–Khalistan War",eyebrow:"War of northern consolidation · 1972–1975",infoboxKicker:"Kashmiri defensive war",flag:consolidationDiagram,
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w3q4r writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:25:02-06:00 -->The author proposes a consequential war between Kashmir and Khalistan during the 1970–85 consolidation period but leaves its victor undecided.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1972–75 Khalistani offensive, Afghan intervention, restoration of most of the prewar line, and permanent Kashmiri entry into Kabul's security orbit constitute the later assistant's compatible working scenario.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w3q4s writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:25:02-06:00 -->A proper Kashmir–Khalistan war capable of changing the western Indian balance is an authorial proposal; its result is explicitly unsettled.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The dates, Khalistani opening attack and objectives, Afghan intervention at Kashmir's request, restoration of most of the line, restricted border, permanent Afghan access, and Afghanistan's strategic victory are compatible transcript development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Belligerent objectives, operation names, battles, commanders, losses, district transfers, settlement, victor, and lasting alignment remain open to authorial selection.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"cause",title:"Working cause",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-x7k2a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:00:00-06:00 --><p>A Khalistani search for depth north of Lahore, control of disputed communities and routes, and protection against Afghan-backed pressure is the assistant's proposed cause.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author establishes only the possibility of a consequential war between Kashmir and Khalistan and leaves its course and result open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"opening",title:"Working Khalistani offensive",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-x7k2b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:00:00-06:00 --><p>Larger Khalistani mobilization, transport, armor, artillery, and shorter routes, followed by an opening seizure of border districts and pressure on roads toward Kotli, form an incorporated campaign scenario.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The attacker, force balance, objectives, and movements are not directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"kashmir",title:"Working Kashmiri defense",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-x7k2c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:00:00-06:00 --><p>Kashmir using mountain approaches but lacking enough depth to defeat Khalistan alone, then requesting an Afghan guarantee and regular support, is incorporated operational extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No request, guarantee, or wartime alignment has been authorially selected.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"afghanistan",title:"Working Afghan intervention",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-x7k2d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:00:00-06:00 --><p>The assistant's scenario has Afghanistan intervene to protect its Peshawar position and regional credibility, supplying ammunition, mountain artillery, aircraft, advisers, transport, volunteers, and regular forces.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author proposes Afghanistan as a stronger western power during this period but does not establish its participation in this war.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"other",title:"Working outside support",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-x7k2e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:00:00-06:00 --><p>Discreet Hindustani and Pakistani aid to Kashmir, limited so that Afghanistan retains coalition leadership, is incorporated diplomatic extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Neither outside supporter nor any coalition structure is directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"settlement",title:"Working settlement",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-x7k2f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:00:00-06:00 --><p>A 1975 settlement restoring most of the prewar line, allowing minor district changes, restricting the frontier, and granting permanent Afghan access and a guarantee of Kashmiri sovereignty is an incorporated scenario.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The date, territorial result, terms, and guarantor remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"winners",title:"Working outcome",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-x7k2g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:00:00-06:00 --><p>Kashmiri survival, expanded Afghan authority, and reduced Khalistani freedom of action describe the assistant's preferred outcome.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author explicitly leaves the victor and resulting balance undecided.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Working legacy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-x7k2h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:00:00-06:00 --><p>A legally independent Kashmir inside an Afghan-led hierarchy, with Khalistan still dangerous and Kabul treated as regional paramount, is compatible extrapolation from the working outcome.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Lasting alignment and regional consequences depend on the still-open result.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"kashmir.html",kicker:"Defending state",label:"Kashmir"},{href:"khalistan.html",kicker:"Attacking state",label:"Khalistan"},{href:"afghanistan.html",kicker:"Intervening guarantor",label:"Kingdom of Afghanistan"},{href:"afghan-protected-frontier.html",kicker:"Strategic background",label:"Afghan-Protected Frontier"}],
    facts:[["Working period","1972–1975"],["Location","Southern and western approaches to Kashmir"],["Working attacker","Khalistan"],["Defender","Kashmir"],["Working intervening power","Afghanistan"],["Kashmiri center","Kotli"],["Authorial outcome","Open"],["Working strategic result","Kashmir enters Afghan security orbit"]]
  }),

  "second-las-bela-war":westernCampaignArticle({
    title:"Second Las Bela War",eyebrow:"Kalat–Balochistan war · 1976–1979",infoboxKicker:"Armored war for the coast",flag:consolidationDiagram,
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l8x9z writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:27:30-06:00 -->The author proposes a second war over Las Bela in which Iranian-supplied Balochistani armor and mechanized forces meet Kalat's newly imported equipment, but explicitly leaves the result undecided.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A 1976–79 Baloch recovery attempt ending with Kalat retaining most of the territory through mounted mobility and quiet Sindhi support is the assistant's compatible working scenario.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l8x9a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:27:30-06:00 -->A second Las Bela conflict involving newly mechanized Kalat and Iranian-equipped Balochistan is authorial canon; its victor is explicitly unsettled.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1976–79 dates, Balochistan offensive plan, force structures, Sindhi support for Kalat, immature mechanized operations, limited Baloch gains, and Kalat retention of the maritime route are compatible transcript development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Orders of battle, campaign phases, battles, equipment models, commanders, losses, armistice, and outcome remain open to authorial selection.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"wound",title:"Las Bela dispute",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s2l6a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:01:00-06:00 --><p><a href='las-bela.html'>Las Bela</a> eventually falls to <a href='kalat.html'>Kalat</a>, giving the khanate a route to the sea; Balochistan wants to reverse that loss but cannot readily push far enough into Kalat to do so.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A specifically named first war, Las Bela's formal founding membership in a confederation, and a Gwadar doctrine of reunification are incorporated framing.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"baloch-force",title:"Proposed Balochistani force",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s2l6b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:01:00-06:00 --><p>The author proposes Iranian-provided armored and mechanized forces for Balochistan in a second Las Bela conflict.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Medium tanks, armored cars, trucks, artillery, radios, fuel, advisers, maintenance, standardized organization, and road-bound operations are incorporated force reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"kalat-force",title:"Proposed Kalat force",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s2l6c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:01:00-06:00 --><p>The author proposes that Kalat may import enough armor and mechanized equipment to become a competent opponent.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A few dozen tanks, carriers, armored cars, and self-propelled guns acquired through Sindhi brokers, Hyderabad, Russian-derived networks, and secondhand markets are incorporated detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"fighting",title:"Proposed immature mechanized fighting",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s2l6d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:01:00-06:00 --><p>Because both sides are new to their equipment, the author expects unusual maneuvers and tactical decisions that might produce stalemate or victory for either side.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Tanks outrunning infantry, road congestion, delayed artillery, failed recovery, vehicle strongpoints, and heavy maintenance losses are compatible operational examples.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"sindh",title:"Open Sindhi policy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s2l6e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:01:00-06:00 --><p>The author leaves <a href='sindh.html'>Sindh's</a> preference unsettled: it may oppose either Baloch claimant becoming dominant, or may prefer a unified Balochistan as a counterweight to Pakistan.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Quiet Sindhi credit, ammunition, spares, intelligence, port access, and contracts for Kalat are the assistant's working choice.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"outcome",title:"Working outcome",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l9a1b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:27:30-06:00 --><p>In the working scenario, Balochistan recovers limited western ground and destroys forward formations but fails to regain the core of Las Bela. Kalat keeps its route to the sea. Iranian support prevents Balochistan's defeat, while Sindhi help prevents Kalat's defeat.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"claims",title:"Working rival claims",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s2l6f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:01:00-06:00 --><p>Balochistan claiming improved capability while Kalat claims successful survival and retained sea access follows from the working stalemate scenario.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No wartime propaganda, legal claim, or postwar procurement conclusion is directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Working legacy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s2l6g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:01:00-06:00 --><p>An armistice that hardens the Las Bela dispute and prepares the later <a href='third-balochistan-war.html'>Third Balochistan War</a> is compatible connective chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The second war's result, settlement, and connection to the directly established early-1980s conflict remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"kalat.html",kicker:"Defending state",label:"Kalat"},{href:"baloch-confederated-state.html",kicker:"Attacking state",label:"Baloch Confederated State"},{href:"sindh.html",kicker:"Covert balance keeper",label:"Sindh"},{href:"third-balochistan-war.html",kicker:"Decisive later conflict",label:"Third Balochistan War"}],
    facts:[["Working period","1976–1979"],["Location","Las Bela and adjoining routes"],["Working attacker","Baloch Confederated State"],["Defender","Kalat"],["Balochistan patron","Iran"],["Working Kalat support","Quiet Sindhi credit, matériel, and access"],["Character","Mechanized contest between the Baloch states"],["Authorial result","Open"]]
  }),

  "hindustan-rajputana-war":westernCampaignArticle({
    title:"Hindustan–Rajputana War",eyebrow:"Conventional war · 1979–1982",infoboxKicker:"War for Delhi's southwestern approaches",flag:consolidationDiagram,
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-r4s5t writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:25:02-06:00 -->The author establishes that Hindustan wins a proper war with Rajputana without necessarily taking large territories.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1979–82 conventional war, defeat of the eastern Rajputana armies, narrow strategic annexation or occupation, demilitarized frontier, nonintervention guarantees, and survival of the princely system form the assistant's compatible working settlement.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-r4s5u writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T00:25:02-06:00 -->A substantial Hindustan–Rajputana war, Hindustani victory, and avoidance of major territorial conquest are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1979–82 dates, offensive objectives, divided Rajputana command, calibrated foreign aid, narrow territorial belt, demilitarized zone, nonintervention guarantee, and subsequent Rajputana reforms are compatible campaign synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Official operation names, battles, commanders, losses, exact settlement, territorial change, and peace title remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"cause",title:"Working cause",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-r7h3a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:03:00-06:00 --><p>Rajputana's proximity to Delhi, hostile transit, mobilization, and possible support for Congress India or Khalistan form the assistant's proposed cause for war.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author establishes a proper Hindustan–Rajputana war but does not state its cause.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"objectives",title:"Working Hindustani objectives",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-r7h3b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:03:00-06:00 --><p>Frontier depth, an end to hostile transit, a demilitarized belt, transport guarantees, and nationalist hopes of absorbing princely India are incorporated Hindustani objectives.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No war aim or public declaration is directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"rajput",title:"Working Rajputana strengths",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-r7h3c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:03:00-06:00 --><p>Fortified cities, local legitimacy, mounted rifles, state armies, foreign equipment, terrain knowledge, and the survival of separate courts after central defeat are compatible strengths of the Rajputana concert.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its force structure and wartime resilience remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"division",title:"Working command divisions",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-r7h3d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:03:00-06:00 --><p>Disagreement among Rajput courts over supreme command, reserves, burdens, and dynastic risk is an incorporated explanation for defeat.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author fixes the Hindustani victory but does not attribute it to a divided Rajputana command.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"aid",title:"Proposed outside assistance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-r7h3e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:03:00-06:00 --><p>The author proposes that other western states prefer Rajputana to win but limit support because they do not want it strong enough to turn those weapons against them.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Congress India, Khalistan, Hyderabad, and specified western states supplying ammunition, aircraft, air defense, intelligence, credit, volunteers, and specialists are incorporated particulars.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"victory",title:"Working explanation for victory",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-r7h3f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:03:00-06:00 --><p>Hindustan wins the war.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Explaining victory through unified command, greater industry, superior tactical aviation, Morozov-derived armor, air defense, engineers, concentrated ammunition, and seizure of eastern positions is incorporated campaign reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"limit",title:"Limited territorial result",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-r7h3g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:03:00-06:00 --><p>The author specifies that Hindustani victory need not involve taking large swaths of territory.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Delhi halting because occupation would require reducing several capitals, holding hostile roads, guarding against Congress India and Khalistan, and risking Hyderabad's intervention is compatible strategic explanation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"settlement",title:"Working settlement",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-r7h3h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:03:00-06:00 --><p>A narrow Hindustani territorial belt, demilitarized zone, nonintervention guarantee, and surviving Rajput dynasties followed by a joint staff, common procurement, shared air warning, and new mobilization rules are incorporated settlement design.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The exact territorial change, treaty, and Rajputana reforms remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"hindustan.html",kicker:"Victorious state",label:"Hindustan"},{href:"rajputana.html",kicker:"Surviving concert",label:"Rajputana"},{href:"princely-balance-of-power.html",kicker:"Strategic background",label:"Princely Balance of Power"},{href:"modern-mounted-warfare-western-india.html",kicker:"Regional military form",label:"Modern Mounted Warfare"}],
    facts:[["Working period","1979–1982"],["Location","Delhi's southwestern approaches and eastern Rajputana"],["Attacker","Hindustan"],["Defender","Rajputana concert"],["Authorial result","Hindustani victory without major conquest"],["Working territorial change","Narrow eastern belt; exact line open"],["Working settlement","Demilitarized zone and nonintervention guarantee"],["Working Rajputana consequence","Stronger common military institutions"]]
  })
});

